@page "/cached-dashboard"

<PageTitle>Cache a shared dashboard · Linq2Dashboard</PageTitle>

<h1>Cache a shared dashboard</h1>

<p>
    The <a href="five-minutes">five-minute app</a> builds its dashboard in the page, on every visit. That is right while the rows are
    few and the user's own: about 10 ms at 10 000 rows. When every user sees the same rows, or there are more than about 100 000 of
    them, build once and share the dashboard instead. It is immutable and thread-safe, so one instance serves every user. This page
    turns the five-minute app into that.
</p>

<h2>1. The cache package</h2>

<p>The service below caches the dashboard in <code>HybridCache</code>.</p>

<pre><code>@PackageExample</code></pre>

<h2>2. The service</h2>

<p>
    Add <code>DashboardService.cs</code> at the project root. Real data is not in memory at startup, so the service loads the rows
    and builds the dashboard the first time a page asks, and caches the result. The definition moves here from the page markup:
    a dashboard shared between pages and users is defined once, where it is built. One instance serves every user until the entry
    expires; the next request after that builds a new one over fresh rows. <code>HybridCache</code> does the hard parts: concurrent
    first requests share one build, and a failed load is not cached. The <code>DisableDistributedCache</code> flag matters: a
    dashboard is an in-memory index and cannot be serialised, so it must never be written to a distributed cache, even when the
    app later gets one.
</p>

<pre><code>@ServiceExample</code></pre>

<p>Register the cache and the service in <code>Program.cs</code>, next to the existing service calls.</p>

<pre><code>@ProgramExample</code></pre>

<h2>3. The page</h2>

<p>
    Replace <code>Components/Pages/Home.razor</code> again. The view now gets the cached <code>Dashboard</code> instead of
    <code>Items</code>, and the components only display what the service defined: the <code>Buckets</code>, <code>Presets</code>,
    <code>Count</code> and <code>Sum</code> parameters are gone. A view renders either a dashboard it was given or one it builds
    itself, never both, so definition parameters under a <code>Dashboard</code> throw; they belong in the builder.
</p>

<pre><code>@PageExample</code></pre>

<h2>4. Run it</h2>

<pre><code>dotnet run</code></pre>

<p>
    The first request waits a second for the stand-in load, then the dashboard appears. Reload the page: it is instant, because the
    cache hands out the dashboard it already built. Blazor also renders the page twice, once for the first HTML and once when the
    circuit connects; the second call gets the cached dashboard too. Clicks, the chips and the URL behave exactly as before.
</p>

<h2>What to change next</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Your data.</strong> Replace the body of <code>LoadOrdersAsync</code> with your query. Build takes about a second per million rows.</li>
    <li><strong>The lifetime.</strong> Ten minutes is a placeholder. Make it as long as your data stays useful, and call <code>ClearAsync</code> from the code that changes the orders so the next request rebuilds at once. If a rebuild must not make anyone wait, build the next one in the background and swap it in when it is ready: <code>DashboardView</code> follows a new <code>Dashboard</code> parameter and keeps the selections.</li>
    <li><strong>Per user or per tenant.</strong> Give each their subset as a scope of the shared dashboard, <code>dashboard.ScopeTo(x =&gt; x.TenantId == tenantId)</code>: it shares the indexes and costs milliseconds, not a build. The <a href="usage">usage</a> guide covers scopes.</li>
    <li><strong>The whole builder.</strong> The <a href="getting-started">getting started</a> page shows labels, top-N with search, date presets, text search and calculated metrics.</li>
</ul>
